busybox-w32: MSYS2-style drive paths and make's flags

CMake's "MSYS Makefiles" generator writes the paths in its makefiles'
commands as MSYS2 spells them, /c/dir for c:/dir, and passes them to the
native programs the commands run (cmake.exe, the compilers), because
MSYS2's runtime converts them on the way, as it does for every native
program an MSYS2 program starts (msys2_path_conv.cc). busybox-w32 did not:
RcppParallel, which names that generator itself, failed under the native
build tools with "sh: /C/.../cmake.exe: not found" (b14-l4 PLAN.md, F14).
GNU make for Windows already hands a command whose program starts with
"/" to $(SHELL) -c. With this patch busybox's sh
  - runs a program named by a drive path (tryexec, in place: same length),
  - changes to one with cd (cdcmd),
  - converts drive paths in a native program's arguments where MSYS2
    would: the whole argument, "-I/c/x", or after '=' (and in an option
    after ',' or ':'), not in arguments with quotes or glob characters,
    and none when MSYS_NO_PATHCONV is set, as MSYS2 (spawnveq; busybox's
    own applets get their arguments unchanged, as MSYS2's own programs).
Only drive letters: there is no MSYS mount table. Unlike MSYS2, a bare
"/c" argument stays: for a native program it is a switch ("cmd /c"), and
busybox's own libtool workaround (tryexec) turns "cmd //c echo" into
"cmd /c echo", which must reach cmd.exe as it is.

And the shell's import of the environment (setwinxp) no longer turns
backslashes into slashes in MAKEFLAGS, MFLAGS and GNUMAKEFLAGS: there a
backslash escapes ("OBJECTS=a.o\ b.o", as make writes a command-line
variable with spaces), and a make the shell starts read "a.o/ b.o" and
failed ("No rule to make target 'b.o/'", RcppParallel's tbb build: make,
sh, R, cmake, make).

Applied by zigbuild/busybox/build.zig (patch.zig) to the release tarball.
r-zig, 2026-11-26.

